<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottawa is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, on the Ottawa river in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. Founded as Bytown in 1826 during the building of the Rideau Canal, it was renamed in 1855 and chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1857 as capital of the Province of Canada, a choice confirmed at Confederation in 1867. The name is often used for the Canadian federal government itself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottawa.jpg" alt="Ottawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arctic.gnome · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament-Ottawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1999 the government of Canada founded the Forum of Federations in <b>Ottawa</b>, an international network through which federal countries compare their experience.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…national anthem after King George VI stood at attention while it was played at the dedication of the National War Memorial in <b>Ottawa</b> on 21 May 1939, following a precedent set by his brother Edward, the previous king, at the Canadian National Vimy Memorial in France in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>On 22 September 1936 Archbishop Joseph-Guillaume-Laurent Forbes banned it from the churches of the Archdiocese of <b>Ottawa</b>, along with other music, to promote Gregorian chant.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li><li><blockquote>…has been taken up far beyond the Muslim world: Olivia Newton-John sang it as an interlude on Grace and Gratitude (2006), and in December 2015, as Syrian refugees first began arriving in Canada, a children's choir in <b>Ottawa</b> sang an arrangement by Laura Hawley at a holiday concert.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna</a></li><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln and Senator Stephen Douglas met in <b>Ottawa</b>, Illinois, for the first of seven debates on the spread of slavery.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States proclaimed the Treaty of St. Louis with the Council of Three Fires, the <b>Ottawa</b>, Ojibwe and Potawatomi of the Illinois and Milwaukee rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
